The StaleElementReferenceException in Selenium NAME_1 occurs when an element on the web page has been updated or removed, but the reference to that element is still being used in the code. This can happen if you try to interact with an element that is no longer present on the page.

To avoid this exception, you can use the following approaches:

1. Store the element in a variable and use the variable in your code instead of directly referencing the element. This way, even if the element is updated or removed from the page, the variable will still contain a reference to it.
2. Use the `wait` method to wait for the element to be available on the page before interacting with it. For example, you can use the `ExpectedConditions` class to wait for the element to be visible, present, or available.
3. Update your code to handle the case where the element is no longer present on the page. For example, you can check if the element is null or if it doesn't match the expected criteria before interacting with it.

Here's an example of how to use the wait method to avoid the StaleElementReferenceException:
```NAME_1
WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();
driver.get("https://www.example.com");

// Wait for the element to be available on the page
WebDriverWait wait = new WebDriverWait(driver, 10);
WebElement element = wait.until(ExpectedConditions.visibilityOfElementLocated(By.id("my-element")));

// Interact with the element
element.sendKeys("Hello world");
```
In this example, we're using the `WebDriverWait` class to wait for 10 seconds for the element with the ID "my-element" to become visible on the page. Once the element is available, we can interact with it by sending keys to it. If the element is no longer present on the page, the `wait.until` method will throw a `TimeoutException`, which is a more appropriate exception to handle in this case.

